# v0.0.4-alpha

This release removes JVM startup from ordinary live edits by reusing a bounded
pool of Java workers. Canonical stays the default; Canonical and raw AST still
compare against the complete correct pool, including the oracle. Behavioral
scores, four example categories, source locators and redacted hints retain their
existing analysis logic.

Identical analyses share pending work or reuse exact-context results. Browser
channels cancel superseded subscriptions, keep another user's shared work intact,
and reject stale results. Retained evidence lets Luna explain displayed hints
and instances without repeating analysis after result-cache eviction. Duplicate
explanation requests also share work. Expired evidence is reported explicitly.

HTTP admission now has finite connection, thread, byte, nesting and deadline
bounds before analysis dispatch. Catalogue/detail APIs support conditional ETag
revalidation. An optional loopback-only health listener has separate capacity.
Worker framing is bounded and identity checked; timeouts/fatal failures retire
workers, and graceful shutdown drains administrator validation and reaps workers.
Public responses and diagnostics continue to exclude credentials and private
reference expressions.

The [implementation report](../performance-implementation.md) records numerical
limits, catalogue differential checks, reproducible commands, deployment details
and proof boundaries. The real browser reuse witness uses three startup JVMs for
two distinct edits and a revisit, with four computations and two cache hits.
All 1,086 fresh/warm feedback comparisons matched across the 181 exercises and
both metrics, including correct-draft zero distances and invalid-input diagnostics.
Local gates passed 378 engine checks, 796 Python tests, 77 browser scenarios and
the additional shutdown, dashboard and administrator checks; release evidence
records final clean-checkout CI and behavioral qualification separately.
Linux/macOS launchers and the private IIS package include the new runtime modules.
Rebuild and install the timestamped IIS ZIP to update an existing deployment;
publishing this source release does not update a production server automatically.

The offline TB01 traffic model gate passes 560 theorem declarations, two clean
builds and 22 witnesses with no admitted proofs or introduced axioms. Production
semantic refinement remains unproved, and the 23 end-to-end traffic obligations
remain formally open. Parsed-reference caching is deferred; whole-process RSS,
OS scheduling and actual IIS deployment acceptance are outside the finite test
claims. Provider tests use disabled or mocked Luna, not paid live API calls.

Public release evidence contains safe aggregate reports and checksums. Deployment
archives, private configuration, credentials and raw reference data are not
published as release assets. Existing `v0.0.2-alpha` and `v0.0.3-alpha` features
and their documented limits remain described in the README.
